\section{Keep Current and Initial Values Separate in Queries}

An invariant sent to a symbolic engine may relate a current
variable to its original value. Store the latter under a
read-only input identifier that never participates in assignment.
For this example take exact integer operations and assume a
location-scoped invariant has been proved for all incoming
states on the given input domain. The query builder receives a
symbolic current store, immutable entry symbols, the current
path condition, and a guard to explore. It must not prune a
feasible candidate because of a mistaken substitution.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Location $\ell$, path $p$, current store $S$, inputs $I$
\Let{A}{\operatorname{checkedInvariant}(\ell)}
\To{Missing evidence contributes no restriction.}
\If{$A$ is absent}
  \Let{A}{\mathrm{true}}
\EndIf
\To{Instantiate both namespaces in one simultaneous substitution.}
\Let{C}{\operatorname{instantiate}(A,\mathrm{current}:S,\mathrm{entry}:I)}
\Let{g}{\operatorname{termForGuard}(S)}
\Let{r}{\operatorname{solve}(p\land C\land g)}
\If{$r$ is proved unsatisfiable}
  \State \Return infeasible guard
\EndIf
\State \Return candidate model or unresolved, according to $r$
\end{sudocode}

Take entry input $N\in[0,9]$ and execute
\texttt{x := input; x := x + 2}. At the next branch,
the proved invariant is $\mathrm{current}(x)=\mathrm{entry}(N)+2$.
Substitution gives $N+2=N+2$. Querying the guard $x=5$
then admits $N=3$, which replays to a current value of $5$.
If an implementation uses the entry symbol for both namespaces,
it will instead conjoin $N=N+2$ and incorrectly prune that run.
Querying $x<2$ with the correct substitution is unsatisfiable,
because $N$ was nonnegative.

The invariant for the builder is that evaluation of the
instantiated expression agrees with evaluation of the original
relation in the concrete current and entry stores of every
modeled run. A cached instantiation must therefore depend on
the symbolic versions, not just on printed names or the
program counter. Renaming a current variable without changing
its binding makes a useful metamorphic test. When using an
approximate symbolic store, a satisfiable model may still be
spurious; concretely replay it. When the solver times out, no
pruning follows.

\section{Information Can Flow in Both Directions}

Alatawi, S{\o}ndergaard, and Miller illustrate how to preserve initial values as extra variables so that relational invariants describe indirect effects of input-dependent looping. An engine can use such an invariant to constrain a summary with fresh values for exit variables. A satisfiable model of that summary is a \emph{candidate} input because the summary may describe impossible executions. Replaying it is part of producing a witness; if replay disagrees, one can refine the summary or explore a more precise prefix. Conversely, checked symbolic infeasibility queries can reveal predicates worth adding to the abstract domain, but merely observing the same fact on many feasible tests does not establish an invariant. Check the refined annotation at entry and on every modeled incoming edge. Check its scope as well: a fact proved assuming a function argument is nonnegative can prune exploration in a caller only if that caller satisfies the premise. Combining the methods is most useful when the implementation distinguishes what has been proved unreachable, what has been observed on a concrete run, and what is still possible only according to an approximation.
